---
import ProjectLayout from '../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro';
---

<ProjectLayout
  title="NBA Player Role Analytics"
  eyebrow="INTERACTIVE D3 ANALYTICS"
  description="An interactive NBA analytics application for comparing statistical roles, historical player-season similarity, roster composition, and team context without reducing players to a single ranking."
  heroImage="/images/projects/nba-role-explorer.png"
  heroAlt="NBA Player Role Analytics Role Explorer comparing Jrue Holiday and James Harden"
  tools="Python · pandas · D3.js · JavaScript · GitHub Pages"
  metrics={[
    { value: '5,665', label: 'eligible player-team-seasons' },
    { value: '480', label: 'team-season records' },
    { value: '16', label: 'NBA seasons' },
    { value: 'Static', label: 'public GitHub Pages app' }
  ]}
  links={[
    {
      label: 'Open Live App',
      href: 'https://ryan-wenstrom-portfolio.github.io/nba-player-role-analytics/'
    },
    {
      label: 'View GitHub',
      href: 'https://github.com/Ryan-Wenstrom-Portfolio/nba-player-role-analytics'
    }
  ]}
>
  <section class="case-section">
    <h2>Problem</h2>
    <div class="stack">
      <p>
        Traditional box-score summaries make it easy to compare points,
        rebounds, and assists, but they often hide how players contribute in
        different statistical roles and team contexts.
      </p>
      <p>
        This project was designed to support comparison rather than declare a
        single “best player.” It keeps player-season roles, historical
        similarity, roster composition, and team winning as descriptive
        context.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <h2>What the app does</h2>
    <div class="two-col">
      <div class="detail-card">
        <h3>Role Explorer</h3>
        <p>
          Compare two player-seasons across scoring load, creation, role
          breadth, efficiency, defensive-event production, historical
          similarity, and team-win context.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="detail-card">
        <h3>Team Roster Lab</h3>
        <p>
          Inspect how a team-season distributes responsibility across its
          rotation and compare different roster shapes rather than individual
          stars alone.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <h2>Data pipeline</h2>
    <div class="pipeline">
      <div class="pipeline-step"><span>01</span><strong>Player stats + salaries</strong></div>
      <div class="pipeline-step"><span>02</span><strong>Game results</strong></div>
      <div class="pipeline-step"><span>03</span><strong>Normalize IDs + eligibility</strong></div>
      <div class="pipeline-step"><span>04</span><strong>Per-36 + role features</strong></div>
      <div class="pipeline-step"><span>05</span><strong>Team-season context</strong></div>
      <div class="pipeline-step"><span>06</span><strong>Static CSV / JSON app data</strong></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <h2>Team context</h2>
    <div class="stack">
      <figure class="supporting-visual">
        <img
          src="/images/projects/nba-team-roster.png"
          alt="NBA Team Roster Lab showing the 2024–25 Oklahoma City Thunder rotation"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>
          Team Roster Lab: role distribution across a selected team-season
          rotation.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <h2>Contribution</h2>
    <div class="note">
      <p>
        The original project was co-developed by Kenny Dolgin and Ryan
        Wenstrom. Both contributed to design, framing, application development,
        evaluation, and presentation. For the portfolio version, Ryan
        restructured the repository, rebuilt the raw-to-app data pipeline,
        validated eligibility and calculations, corrected team-season records,
        regenerated application data, cleaned deployment, and expanded
        methodology and recruiter-facing documentation.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <h2>Responsible interpretation</h2>
    <div class="stack">
      <p>
        Role breadth describes statistical profile, not overall player quality.
        Historical similarity is not a ranking, and team win percentage is
        descriptive context rather than proof that a player or role caused a
        team to win.
      </p>

      <figure class="supporting-visual">
        <img
          src="/images/projects/nba-methodology.png"
          alt="NBA Player Role Analytics About and Methodology page"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>
          The public app includes explicit methodology, audience, source, and
          interpretation guidance.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>
</ProjectLayout>
